// Viṣṇu Sahasranāma — concepts 401–500 (English, own paraphrases).
// Titles are IAST nominatives in Śaṅkara-tradition numbering; summaries
// condense the Śaṅkara–Satyabhāṣyam readings in this project's own words.
// See vishnu-sahasranama-source-provenance.ts.
export const vsConcepts401500: any[] = [
  { id: "vs-nama-401", title: "Vīraḥ", category: "Lotus-Resolve", summary: "Valour personified (named again). Vīra is courage embodied rather than merely exercised: bravery with a face, steadiness that can be approached and imitated. The verse stations valour after guidance-names (led, leader, unleaded), so courage reads as direction kept under pressure rather than impulse. Repetition is itself doctrine: strength must be re-announced wherever dharma is defined. Embody one courage today, quietly and completely. (Stotra verse 43.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-043"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-402", title: "Śaktimatāṃ śreṣṭhaḥ", category: "Lotus-Resolve", summary: "Best among the powerful. Śaktimatāṃ-śreṣṭha superlatives strength's hierarchy: the strong have a strongest, powers a peak, and every battery of force terminates upward in Him. Eminence among eminences teaches humility inside power — even the mighty have a Mightier. The verse pairs this with dharma (next names) so that supremacy never reads as mere muscle. Peak once today powerfully, then bow upward. (Stotra verse 43.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-043"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-403", title: "Dharmaḥ", category: "Lotus-Resolve", summary: "Dharma itself, the law of being. Dharma is existence's grain: the way things are and the way they ought to be in one word, order as both fact and command. Valour and supremacy (previous names) resolve here — strength exists to serve right, not to replace it. Śaṅkara's lineage reads dharma as the support that supports even its supporters. Grain once today rightly: act with things' grain, not against it. (Stotra verse 43.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-043"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-404", title: "Dharmaviduttamaḥ", category: "Lotus-Resolve", summary: "Best of dharma-knowers. Dharmavid-uttama perfects jurisprudence: law known utterly, rightly applied, never merely recited — knowing completed in doing. After dharma itself (previous name), this names mastery of it: the Law has a Knower, and knowing has a summit. The verse closes guidance in legislation perfected, so the whole Rāma-arc ends under law. Know one law best today by practising it exactly. (Stotra verse 43.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-043"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-405", title: "Vaikuṇṭhaḥ", category: "Lotus-Resolve", summary: "Vaikuṇṭha, the flawless expanse where straying ends. Vi-kuṇṭha denies bluntness, bending, failure: beings kept from going astray, wandering intercepted before it begins. The verse opens the breath-and-Om arc with destination first — expanse before narrowing — so that meditation knows its address before it starts walking. Error prevented is grace prior to effort. Stay once today unstrayed, returning early rather than late. (Stotra verse 44.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-044"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-406", title: "Puruṣaḥ", category: "Lotus-Resolve", summary: "The Person dwelling in all bodies (named again). Puruṣa restates indwelling inside the expanse: the flawless vast (previous name) is tenanted personally, not empty — infinity inhabited, vastness with a face. Repetition places tenancy at meditation's centre: the expanse narrows to a Person before narrowing further to breath. No frame lacks its Tenant. Reaffirm one indwelling today: greet the Person in one body. (Stotra verse 44.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-044"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-407", title: "Prāṇaḥ", category: "Lotus-Resolve", summary: "Vital life-force itself (named again). Prāṇa is breath as being's wage, paid moment by moment: vitality reissued to the faint, existence inflated like lungs. After expanse and Person, this narrows meditation to the most intimate handle — your own breathing. The tradition reads prāṇa as the first god to be thanked each morning, since waking is re-grant. Reissue one breath today gratefully, feeling it lent. (Stotra verse 44.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-044"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-408", title: "Prāṇadaḥ", category: "Lotus-Resolve", summary: "Giver of life-breath (named again). Prāṇada turns vitality into gift: existence granted daily, breathing donated rather than owned, each inhalation a fresh issue from His treasury. The Giver stands beside the gift (previous name) so that breath never reads as mechanism — physiology as generosity. Prayer leans on this giving: petition addresses One already giving without petition. Re-grant one living thing today: feed, water, or encourage. (Stotra verse 44.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-044"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-409", title: "Praṇavaḥ", category: "Lotus-Resolve", summary: "The syllable Om, praised by gods. Praṇava is sound's source and speech's seed: utterance ultimate, the bow from which all mantras are arrows, as the Muṇḍaka images it. Breath (previous names) utters here — vitality flowering into sacred sound — and the verse's whole arc pivots on this syllable: expanse narrowed to Om before widening again. Sound Om once slowly today as address, not mere syllable. (Stotra verse 44.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-044"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-410", title: "Pṛthuḥ", category: "Lotus-Resolve", summary: "The immense, vast without threat. Pṛthu is size that shelters: vastness hospitable, magnitude maternal — the king Pṛthu's earth-milking stands behind the name, abundance drawn forth by righteous demand. After Om's concentration, immensity re-opens: meditation narrows to a syllable, then widens to shelter. Guarded vastness follows (foe-slaying next), so openness never means exposure. Shelter one small thing vastly today. (Stotra verse 44.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-044"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-411", title: "Hiraṇyagarbhaḥ", category: "Lotus-Resolve", summary: "Golden-womb keeper of the source (named again). Hiraṇyagarbha re-gilds origin: beginning's gold re-burnished, the shining egg of worlds recalled mid-meditation so that vastness (previous name) has a bright centre. The Vedic golden germ (Ṛgveda 10.121) is creation's dawn remembered at noon. Every vastness needs a shining heart, lest expanse feel empty. Re-burnish one beginning today: recall an origin gratefully. (Stotra verse 44.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-044"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-412", title: "Śatrughnaḥ", category: "Lotus-Resolve", summary: "Destroyer of enemies. Śatrughna ends opposition: foes finished, hostility halted, the open vast (previous names) guarded so that shelter never becomes exposure. Destruction here is protection's edge — weeds pulled, not gardens burned — and the tradition hears Bharata's brother in the name: loyalty fighting in righteousness' cause. Foe-slaying guards vastness, the verse insists. End one hostility today, outer or inner. (Stotra verse 44.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-044"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-413", title: "Vyāptaḥ", category: "Lotus-Resolve", summary: "The all-pervading, cause soaking effects. Vyāpta is pervasion causal: products permeated by their producer, pots soaked in clay, waves in ocean — effects never leaving their cause. After foe-slaying, pervasion reassures: what was defended is already filled, guardianship inside tenancy. Śaṅkara's lineage stresses cause-pervasion against mere space-pervasion: He fills by originating. Soak once today causally: trace one effect to its Source. (Stotra verse 44.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-044"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-414", title: "Vāyuḥ", category: "Lotus-Resolve", summary: "Air wafting fragrance. Vāyu carries sweetness: scent borne, atmosphere sweetened, the pervader (previous name) given motion and perfume. Breath cosmic after breath personal — the verse's prāṇa opened inwardly, vāyu opens outwardly — and the tradition smells grace in fragrance: goodness arriving before it is seen. Breeze is doctrine: presence unfelt but sweet. Spread one sweetness today invisibly, like wind. (Stotra verse 44.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-044"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-415", title: "Adhokṣajaḥ", category: "Lotus-Resolve", summary: "Beyond senses' reach, vitality unfailing. Adhokṣaja — below (adhas) the axle-senses' decline (kṣaya avoided) — names transcendence tonic: ever fresh, never jaded, unworn by being used. The verse that narrowed from expanse to Om widens past grasp here: meditation's whole journey ends beyond instruments. Below-axle yet above-grasp, the senses' Lord exceeds their ledger. Freshen once today transcendently: touch something worn as though new. (Stotra verse 44.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-044"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-416", title: "Ṛtuḥ", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "The seasons themselves. Ṛtu is time tempered: the year-wheel turning, seasons succeeding in order — spring, rains, harvest — each arriving on appointment. The altar-arc opens with calendar because sacrifice runs on seasons: rites have dates, and dates have a Lord. Regularity is mercy: the world changing predictably rather than collapsing randomly. Turn once today seasonally: keep one rhythm as worship. (Stotra verse 45.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-045"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-417", title: "Sudarśanaḥ", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Fair to see, easily seen by devotees. Sudarśana is visibility gracious: sight granted, vision vouchsafed — the fair form that lets itself be beheld, and the discus that defends beholders, both in one word. After seasons (previous name), fairness teaches that time's face can be beautiful, not only terrible (ugra follows). The highest seat embraces by showing itself. See once today grantedly: look until fairness appears. (Stotra verse 45.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-045"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-418", title: "Kālaḥ", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Time the reckoner. Kāla measures and audits: moments counted, ages closed, every loan of days called in at last. Between the fair (previous name) and the terrible (next), time wears both faces — season's kindness and year's severity one reckoning. The Gītā's world-consuming time stands behind this name, yet here auditing serves the altar: sacrifice needs appointed hours. Audit one moment today honestly. (Stotra verse 45.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-045"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-419", title: "Parameṣṭhī", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Centered in supreme glory, seated highest. Parameṣṭhin is enthronement essential: established uttermost, posted at the summit — yet the verse makes the highest seat embrace (next name) rather than exclude. Height as shelter: supremacy that gathers instead of looming. Every hierarchy terminates upward in welcome. Seat once today supremely: take the high view that includes, not the one that despises. (Stotra verse 45.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-045"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-420", title: "Parigrahaḥ", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "The receiver, embracer all around. Parigraha takes in: all accepted, none turned away — grasping around (pari-grah) as encompassing, not clutching. After the highest seat (previous name), embracing teaches that height stoops to hold: enthronement expressing as hospitality. What the seasons bring and time audits, He receives. Take in once today amply: include someone the circle usually drops. (Stotra verse 45.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-045"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-421", title: "Ugraḥ", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "The formidable, terrible in aspect. Ugra is awe's face: terror hallowing, dread dignifying — fierceness that consecrates rather than merely frightens. The verse needs this severity between embracing and the year: time's fairness (sudarśana) completed by time's terror, grace total only with gravity. Rudra's awe stands inside Viṣṇu's names on purpose. Dread once today dignifiedly: let one fear make you careful, not cowardly. (Stotra verse 45.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-045"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-422", title: "Saṃvatsaraḥ", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "The year, in whom all things reside (named again). Saṃvatsara houses beings in time's hostel: residence annual, the twelve-month circuit as lodging — all that lives lodged in His circling. After terror (previous name), the year reassures: awfulness wheels inside hospitality, severity scheduled inside shelter. Time's hostel has a Keeper. House once today yearly: give one transient thing a season's lodging. (Stotra verse 45.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-045"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-423", title: "Dakṣaḥ", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Efficient, the quick completer. Dakṣa is dexterity divine: works finished fast and flawlessly, skill without fumbling — the Prajāpati Dakṣa's name restored to its divine owner. Between the year and rest (next name), deftness teaches that time's vastness wastes nothing: every season used, every hour tooled. Competence is compassion in work-clothes. Finish one work today flawlessly and promptly. (Stotra verse 45.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-045"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-424", title: "Viśrāmaḥ", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Giver of rest. Viśrāma lays labour down: toil tucked in, weariness housed — repose granted, not merely permitted. After deftness (previous name), rest completes work: efficiency earning evening, the year's wheel pausing at dusk. The tradition reads rest as His gift rather than His need — He who never tires gives tiredness its haven. Tuck in one toil today: rest deliberately, without guilt. (Stotra verse 45.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-045"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-425", title: "Viśvadakṣiṇaḥ", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Most skillful toward all. Viśvadakṣiṇa directs dexterity outward: skill serving every side, competence caring — deftness (dakṣa) universalized into impartial helpfulness. The verse closes here so that time's whole arc — seasons, terror, year, rest — ends in skill that serves all without exception. Sacrifice's fee (dakṣiṇā) puns inside: every rite's payment is His all-round competence. Serve skilfully once today, all-roundly. (Stotra verse 45.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-045"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-426", title: "Vistāraḥ", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Expander of the world. Vistāra spreads spacious: extension endless, roominess righteous — creation unfolded, elbow-room issued to beings. The seed-verse opens with spreading before steadying (pillar next): vastness first, stability inside it. Expansion is hospitality: making room is the first mercy. Room once today spaciously: make space for someone crowded out. (Stotra verse 46.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-046"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-427", title: "Sthāvarasthāṇuḥ", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Pillar of the immovable. Sthāvarasthāṇu stations stillness: fixity founded, stability stacked — the post to which sacrifice is tied, the axis that does not turn. After expansion (previous name), the pillar teaches that vastness needs anchoring: room held open by firmness. Movement moves around the unmoving. Stack one stability today: fix a single habit-post. (Stotra verse 46.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-046"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-428", title: "Pramāṇam", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Right measure and knowledge personified. Pramāṇa is proof embodied: evidence ensouled, warrant walking — the valid means of knowing (perception, inference, scripture) gathered into a Person. Between pillar and seed, measure portions: stability dispensed by rule, expansion engineered. The logicians' pride becomes devotion's Lord: reasoning His shape. Warrant once today walkingly: prove one claim fairly. (Stotra verse 46.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-046"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-429", title: "Bījamavyayam", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "The immutable seed — one name, bīja joined to avyaya. Germination guaranteed: sprouting secured, growth gilt-edged — the seed that never decays though harvests come and go. Measure (previous name) plants here: what warrant portions, the seed secures. The Gītā's imperishable seed (IX.18) stands behind this compound. Plant one seed-purpose today patiently, trusting its decaylessness. (Stotra verse 46.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-046"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-430", title: "Arthaḥ", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Purpose itself, worshipped goal of all. Artha is meaning meant: sense sought, significance signed — wealth, aim and sense in one word, for what enriches also directs. After the seed (previous name), purpose sprouts: germination given direction. Even purposelessness (next name) will be His, but purpose comes first. Signify once today significantly: give one act a clear aim. (Stotra verse 46.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-046"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-431", title: "Anarthaḥ", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Beyond purpose, yet held — purposelessness owned. Anartha denies utility to the Unlimited: even the unmeaning meaningfully held, meaningless stretches belonging somewhere. Paired with artha (previous name), this holds both: nothing wasted, not even waste — fallow fields also His acreage. The verse's mercy peaks here: failure has an Owner. Hold one unmeaning stretch today without despising it. (Stotra verse 46.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-046"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-432", title: "Mahākośaḥ", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "The great treasury. Mahākośa reserves riches: wealth warehoused, worth stored — sheaths (kośa) of being, from food to bliss, all stocked in Him. After purpose and purposelessness, the treasury funds both: meaning and fallow alike provisioned. Abundance with roots, the verse insists — treasury before enjoyment. Store one worth today: save something good instead of spending it. (Stotra verse 46.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-046"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-433", title: "Mahābhogaḥ", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Of great enjoyment by nature. Mahābhoga is bliss constitutional: delight as being, joy as nature — not pleasure visited but paradise inhabited. Treasury (previous name) opens into enjoyment: stored worth enjoyed greatly, riches relished, not merely kept. The world is His banquet, not His burden. Joy once today naturally: enjoy one stored good fully. (Stotra verse 46.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-046"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-434", title: "Mahādhanaḥ", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Of great wealth. Mahādhana owns opulence outright: riches regal, fortune freehold — abundance as attribute, not accident. The verse closes treasury-enjoyment-wealth as abundance rooted: seed, purpose, store, delight, fortune one growth. Greatness swallows hoarding: wealth worn lightly because inexhaustible. Own one fortune today freely: hold plenty without clutching. (Stotra verse 46.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-046"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-435", title: "Anirviṇṇaḥ", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Never dejected, without discontent. Anirviṇṇa is cheer constitutional: gloom's impossibility, despair's denial — the Lord never desponds over creation's mess, never regrets the project. The contraries-verse opens here so that massive-yet-thin, patient-yet-yearning all rest on undejection. Devotion borrows this climate: approach the Never-dejected and lift. Deny one despair today: refuse a single dejection. (Stotra verse 47.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-047"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-436", title: "Sthaviṣṭhaḥ", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Immense, most massive (named again). Sthaviṣṭha re-masses hugeness: vastness re-heaped mid-contraries, grossness magnified past conceiving precisely so subtlety may dawn by contrast. After undejection (previous name), mass reassures: cheer has bulk, joy has weight — not froth but mountain. The huge is the ladder to the minute. Honour one hugeness today: feel small gladly before something great. (Stotra verse 47.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-047"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-437", title: "Abhūḥ", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "The birthless, who never came to be. Abhū bypasses becoming: beginning declined, origination refused — unborn-ness utter, not merely unrecorded. Among contraries (massive yet thin) this stills the anxiety of start: what never began cannot end, and devotion rests on ground that never broke ground. The unborn anchors the massive. Decline one compulsive beginning today: let something stay unstarted in peace. (Stotra verse 47.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-047"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-438", title: "Dharmayūpaḥ", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Sacrificial post of dharma. Dharmayūpa stakes righteousness upright: law lifted high as the post to which sacrifice is tied, standard raised so wanderers can steer. The unborn (previous name) gives antiquity a pole: timelessness marked visibly in time. Every rite circles this post; every oath touches it. Lift one standard today: raise a single right practice visibly. (Stotra verse 47.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-047"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-439", title: "Mahāmakhaḥ", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "The great sacrifice itself. Mahāmakha is offering immense: worship vast, oblation oceanic — rite magnified past measure until sacrifice becomes cosmos. Post (previous name) serves rite: the stake exists for the offering, standard for the surrender. Grandeur here is not display but capacity: room for every gift. Offer once today oceanically: give something larger than comfort. (Stotra verse 47.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-047"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-440", title: "Nakṣatranemiḥ", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Hub of the star-wheel. Nakṣatranemi centres cosmos: stellar traffic regulated from this nave, constellations wheeling on His axle. Sacrifice (previous names) turns celestial here: rites below, stars above, one nave. Stability cosmic makes stability personal: your circling also has a hub. Centre once today cosmically: refer one circling worry to the Nave. (Stotra verse 47.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-047"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-441", title: "Nakṣatrī", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Chief of stars, moon-crowned. Nakṣatrin commands constellations: asterisms administered, lunar mansions marshalled — the nave (previous name) given a face, centrality wearing light. Night's government is personal: stars have a Chief, darkness has an administration. The verse pairs hub with chief so order never reads as machinery. Administer one small asterism of duties today neatly. (Stotra verse 47.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-047"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-442", title: "Kṣamaḥ", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Ever patient, forbearing. Kṣama endures: tolerance enthroned, forbearance fixed — offence absorbed without tremor, delay accepted without sulk. Among contraries (massive yet thin), patience steadies: stars wheel slowly, sacrifice ripens slowly, and yearning (next names) waits. The tradition counts patience creation's climate: worlds ripen, not snap. Enthrone one tolerance today: absorb a single offence. (Stotra verse 47.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-047"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-443", title: "Kṣāmaḥ", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Thin, the slender remainder after the deluge. Kṣāma survives slender: essence enduring when bulk dissolves, the seed-thin remnant from which worlds restart. Beside patience (previous name) and the massive (name 436), thinness completes the contraries: huge and slight one Lord, bulk and remnant one mercy. Reduction can be preservation. Endure essentially once today: survive one loss as seed. (Stotra verse 47.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-047"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-444", title: "Samīhanaḥ", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Whose longings are auspicious. Samīhana yearns benignly: wants well-wishing, desires blessing — the verse's shock-ending, cosmic stability that still longs for devotees. After patience and thinness, yearning teaches that perfection is not indifference: the Complete still reaches, the Full still wishes. Stars wheel, yet the Hub yearns. Yearn once today blessingfully: want someone's good ardently. (Stotra verse 47.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-047"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-445", title: "Yajñaḥ", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Sacrifice personified. Yajña is offering embodied: worship walking, oblation alive — rite not performed by Him but as Him. The knowledge-arc opens with act so that wisdom (verse's end) has a road: ritual first, ripening later. Every offering terminates in His acceptance; every giving-away arrives. Walk worshipfully once today: do one act as offering. (Stotra verse 48.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-048"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-446", title: "Ijyaḥ", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Fit to be invoked through sacrifice. Ijya deserves calling: invocation merited, worship warranted — the worthy object of every altar's address. After sacrifice itself (previous name), this names its direction: offering has an Address, worship a Worthy. The tradition stresses fitness: not flattery but recognition — He is invoked because invocation suits Him. Crown one calling today: invoke Him deliberately. (Stotra verse 48.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-048"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-447", title: "Mahejyaḥ", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Worthy of supreme worship. Mahejya superlatives invocation: adoration apex, reverence uttermost — worship's summit, honour's highest degree. Ijya (previous name) ascends here: fitting worship becomes greatest worship, recognition ripening into rapture. The verse climbs act-object-rite-session toward knowledge; this is devotion's high camp. Honour once today highest: give your best, not your remainder. (Stotra verse 48.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-048"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-448", title: "Kratuḥ", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "The solemn rite, śrauta ceremony. Kratu is ritual weighty: observance honoured, ceremony grave — Vedic solemnity with fixed altars and measured chant. After supreme worship (previous name), solemnity disciplines rapture: feeling given form, devotion engineered to last. Form carries fervour past mood's expiry. Observe once today gravely: keep one form exactly. (Stotra verse 48.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-048"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-449", title: "Satram", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "The long session, protector of the good. Satra extends rite into duration: worship weeks-long, goodness guarded across time — sacrifice as marathon, not moment. Solemnity (previous name) lengthens here: gravity sustained until it becomes climate. The good need guarding, and duration guards. Guard one goodness long today: persist in a single right practice. (Stotra verse 48.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-048"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-450", title: "Satāṃ gatiḥ", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Goal of the good, salvation-seekers' destination (named again). Satāṃ-gati re-destines: the Good's goal re-goaled, journey's end re-announced mid-ritual so that means never eclipse end. Sacrifice entire — act, object, rite, session — walks toward this Address. Rites are roads; arrival is a Person. Re-goal once today: check one practice against its destination. (Stotra verse 48.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-048"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-451", title: "Sarvadarśī", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "All-seeing, all-knowing witness. Sarvadarśin sees through: sights seen utterly, shows shown up — vision veilless, nothing curtained. After the goal (previous name), all-seeing reassures pilgrims: the Destination watches the road, every step noticed, no sincere walker unseen. Seeing here frees rather than spies (verse theme): regard that liberates. See through once today kindly: notice someone fully. (Stotra verse 48.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-048"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-452", title: "Vimuktātmā", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Soul free of bondages. Vimuktātman is freedom final: fetters fallen, ties untied — the Self unbound by nature, and by remembrance unbinding rememberers. All-seeing (previous name) frees: vision completing in liberation, the witness graduating its watched. Sacrifice's graduate stands here: ritual's end is release. Untie one tie today: drop a single bondage deliberately. (Stotra verse 48.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-048"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-453", title: "Sarvajñaḥ", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Omniscient, knowing all. Sarvajña knows knowingly: knowledge knowing itself, wisdom wise about wisdom — past, future, hidden, subtle all open. After freedom (previous name), omniscience teaches that release is not blankness but fullness of knowing. The freed-souled knows because He is knowledge's source. Know once today knowingly: learn something as though studying Him. (Stotra verse 48.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-048"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-454", title: "Jñānamuttamam", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Supreme knowledge — one name, jñāna joined to uttama. Wisdom's summit: understanding uttermost, insight innermost — knowledge that liberates rather than merely informs. The verse resolves here: sacrifice ripened into wisdom, ritual's whole arc fruited. Act, object, rite, session, goal, vision, freedom, omniscience — all harvested as knowing. Insight once today innermost: convert one rite into understanding. (Stotra verse 48.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-048"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-455", title: "Suvrataḥ", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Fair-vowed, pure in resolve. Suvrata keeps pledges pristine: vows virtuous, promises polished — resolve beautiful because resolve is kept. The sixfold su-arc opens with vow: auspiciousness beginning in commitment, sweetness rooted in seriousness. Beauty without backbone is decoration; vow gives fairness a spine. Polish one promise today: keep a single vow exactly. (Stotra verse 49.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-049"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-456", title: "Sumukhaḥ", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Fair-faced, charming in countenance. Sumukha captivates kindly: face fascinating, presence pleasing — beauty that welcomes rather than dazzles. After the fair vow (previous name), fair face teaches that resolve sweetens features: kept promises bloom visibly. The tradition reads the face as vow's fruit — inward fidelity flowering outward. Please once today presently: meet someone face-first with gladness. (Stotra verse 49.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-049"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-457", title: "Sūkṣmaḥ", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Subtlest source of creation. Sūkṣma is fineness fundamental: minuteness mighty, smallness supreme — cause finer than effect, atom's atom, the Kaṭha's inward journey past sheaths to the Self. Fairness (previous names) deepens here: beauty's root is subtlety, charm's ground minuteness. What is finest founds the vast. Honour one smallness today: attend to something minute. (Stotra verse 49.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-049"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-458", title: "Sughoṣaḥ", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Sweet-voiced, lord of auspicious sound. Sughoṣa tunes resonance righteous: tones tuned, noises nobled — utterance musical, proclamation pleasant. Subtlety (previous name) sounds here: fineness flowering as harmony, depth speaking sweetly. The Vedas are His voice, the tradition says; thunder is also His, but tuned. Noble one noise today: sweeten a single utterance. (Stotra verse 49.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-049"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-459", title: "Sukhadaḥ", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Giver of happiness. Sukhada grants gladness: joy given, delight dealt — happiness distributed as policy, beatitude administered. Sweetness (previous names) gives here: sound ripening into joy, fairness fruited as gladness. The bestower assures where the terrible (ugra) awed: the same power, faced as benefactor. Deal one delight today: give a concrete gladness. (Stotra verse 49.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-049"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-460", title: "Suhṛt", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Friend of all beings, without exception. Suhṛd is amity absolute: enmity's end, goodwill's all — the good-hearted (su-hṛd) whose heart trends kind toward every creature. Six su-names melt here: auspiciousness becoming intimacy, fairness ripening into friendship. The Gītā's friend of all beings (V.29) stands behind this name. Befriend one being today without agenda. (Stotra verse 49.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-049"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-461", title: "Manoharaḥ", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Captivator of minds. Manohara arrests attention: thoughts taken, minds moved — beauty that steals thinking the way Kṛṣṇa stole hearts in Vraja. After friendship (previous name), captivation teaches that affection fascinates: love holds attention without chaining it. Mind-theft merciful: stolen for its own good. Move one mind today kindly: fascinate someone toward the good. (Stotra verse 49.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-049"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-462", title: "Jitakrodhaḥ", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Conqueror of anger. Jitakrodha has felled fury: temper tamed, rage ruled — victory inward before arms outward (hero-arms follow). Strength with temper mastered: the verse arms heroism only after anger is conquered, so power never serves pique. Wrath conquered is wrath available — energy freed for justice. Rule one rage today: master a single flare. (Stotra verse 49.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-049"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-463", title: "Vīrabāhuḥ", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Mighty-armed hero. Vīrabāhu shoulders strength: arms heroic, power plated — might moulded, force proportioned. After anger conquered (previous name), arms are safe: heroism with temper mastered, strength that can be trusted with edge. Arms aesthetic and ethical at once — shapely because self-ruled. Plate one power today kindly: put strength behind a weak thing. (Stotra verse 49.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-049"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-464", title: "Vidāraṇaḥ", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Cleaver of the unrighteous. Vidāraṇa cuts knots: tangles torn, blockages split — cleaving that cleanses, surgery not butchery. Hero-arms (previous names) wield here: mastered strength cutting precisely, anger-conquered force dividing false from true. The verse ends in discrimination armed: sweetness can sever. Tear one tangle today: cut a single knot cleanly. (Stotra verse 49.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-049"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-465", title: "Svāpanaḥ", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Luller to sleep. Svāpana sends slumber: repose rained, rest given — the weary tucked in by One who never tires. The tenderness-verse opens with sleep so that multiplicity (many souls, many deeds) is met rested: metaphysics for the refreshed, not the exhausted. Rest is His gift, not His need. Rain one repose today: let someone rest without guilt. (Stotra verse 50.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-050"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-466", title: "Svavaśaḥ", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Self-controlled, under own sway. Svavaśa is mastery inward: ruled from within, reined by Self — dependence on none, obedience only to own nature. The luller (previous name) needs no lulling: rest given by One who needs none, calm dealt from fullness. Autonomy perfect teaches devotion's goal: reined from inside, not yanked from outside. Rein once today inwardly. (Stotra verse 50.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-050"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-467", title: "Vyāpī", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "All-pervading. Vyāpin spreads sheer: extension everywhere, presence pandemic in the old good sense — all steeped, nothing exempt. Self-rule (previous name) spreads here: mastery within expressing as presence throughout, autonomy flowering into ubiquity. Pervasion is not occupation but intimacy. Spread once today sheerly: be present somewhere unattended. (Stotra verse 50.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-050"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-468", title: "Naikātmā", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Many-souled, assuming various forms. Naikātman multiplies selves: persons proliferated, all Him — unity wearing multiplicity the way light wears colours. Pervasion (previous name) personalizes here: spread becoming selves, everywhere becoming someones. No presentation foreign; each a dialect of presence. Proliferate one kindness today variously: help in several small ways. (Stotra verse 50.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-050"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-469", title: "Naikakarmakṛt", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Doer of many deeds. Naikakarmakṛt acts abundantly: works manifold, undertakings uncounted — multiplicity administered from unity, busyness without distraction. Many selves (previous name) work here: persons proliferated into labours, presence expressing as service. Action without adhesion: doing all, owned by none. Act once today abundantly: do several small goods. (Stotra verse 50.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-050"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-470", title: "Vatsaraḥ", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Abode of everything. Vatsara houses whole: all lodged, each chambered — the year (vatsara) as hostel, time's circuit as lodging. Many deeds (previous name) lodge here: multiplicity sheltered in unity's inn. Every wanderer has quarters; every season has rooms. The verse softens toward calf-tenderness next: housing ripening into fondness. Lodge one all today: shelter something whole. (Stotra verse 50.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-050"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-471", title: "Vatsalaḥ", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Tender lover of devotees. Vatsala is affection calf-deep: fondness following like a cow her calf, love lowing — the cowherd's tenderness in metaphysical dress. Abode (previous name) warms here: housing becoming fondness, shelter sweetening into love. The tradition hears Kṛṣṇa's pasture in this name: omnipotence gone pastoral. Low once today lovingly: follow someone's need tenderly. (Stotra verse 50.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-050"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-472", title: "Vatsī", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Calf-possessing protector of herds. Vatsin keeps calves: crowds covered, the young guarded — possession as protection, ownership as guardianship. Tenderness (previous name) takes charge here: fondness shouldering responsibility, affection standing watch. Every calf-creature has a Keeper; every crowd a Herdsman. Keep one calf-creature today: guard something young. (Stotra verse 50.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-050"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-473", title: "Ratnagarbhaḥ", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Jewel-wombed ocean holding gems. Ratnagarbha depths with treasure: riches reefed, valuables trenched — the sea concealing pearls, being concealing worth. Herding (previous names) enriches here: kept things prove precious, guarded growth yields gems. Concealment is mercy: treasure buried for finding. Trench one treasure today: hide a good deed unadvertised. (Stotra verse 50.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-050"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-474", title: "Dhaneśvaraḥ", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Lord of wealth. Dhaneśvara rules riches righteously: fortune's lord, fund's father — opulence administered, not hoarded. Jewel-depths (previous name) gain a treasurer: treasure with governance, plenty with principle. Kubera's title restored upward: every fund has a Father. Father one fund today: administer some resource fairly. (Stotra verse 50.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-050"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-475", title: "Dharmagup", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Protector of dharma — this edition's reading (Śaṅkara's text) against dharmabhuk elsewhere, recorded not smoothed over. Guarding precedes making and embodying (next names): law looked after before lived or incarnated. Protection is priority: justice jacketed, rightness warded. The verse opens law's triad with shelter. Jacket one justice today: protect a single right practice. (Stotra verse 51.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-051"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-476", title: "Dharmakṛt", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Maker of dharma, acting according to right. Dharmakṛt lives law: justice journeyed, rightness done — ordinance authored and obeyed in one motion. After guarding (previous name), making teaches that protection works by example: shelter through enactment. The Lawgiver keeps His own law, authorizing obedience. Journey one justice today: enact a right you usually only praise. (Stotra verse 51.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-051"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-477", title: "Dharmī", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Dharmic by nature, supporter of dharma. Dharmin roots rightness: law as life, justice as juice — being dharmic rather than merely doing dharma. Guarding and making (previous names) ground here: protection and enactment flowing from essence. Character, not compliance: the root that fruits as law. Juice one justice today: let a right act flow from nature, not effort. (Stotra verse 51.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-051"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-478", title: "Sat", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Being itself, eternal truth — counted first of the verse's four (sat, asat, kṣara, akṣara). Sat is is-ness bare: existence raw, reality real — the existent that never non-exists. Dharma's triad (previous names) opens onto metaphysics here: law outranks logic's pairs, so being is named inside righteousness. Rest once in bare is-ness today without predicates. (Stotra verse 51.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-051"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-479", title: "Asat", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Non-being, also held — second of the four. Asat owns unreality: the unreal also His, night also nuanced — illusion's stuff still His stuff, absence still addressed. Paired with sat (previous name), this refuses dualism: being and non-being one Lordship, day and night one House. Error has an Owner, and ownership redeems. Nuance one night today: find His hand in one darkness. (Stotra verse 51.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-051"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-480", title: "Kṣaram", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "The perishable — third of the four. Kṣara permits passing: flux allowed, flow sanctioned — what melts, breaks, ends, all let to pass through His hands. After being/non-being, perishable/imperishable re-states the pairs in time's key: ontology becoming calendar. Passing is permitted, not merely suffered. Sanction one flow today: let something end gracefully. (Stotra verse 51.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-051"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-481", title: "Akṣaram", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "The imperishable — fourth of the four. Akṣara pairs permanence with flux: passing's opposite owned equally, the undiminishing completing the diminishing. The Gītā's two puruṣas (XV.16) stand behind this pair: kṣara and akṣara both, then Puruṣottama beyond. Wholeness holds contraries without splitting. Pair once today permanently: set one imperishable beside a passing thing. (Stotra verse 51.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-051"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-482", title: "Avijñātā", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Unrecognized, beyond knowing's grasp. Avijñātṛ owns mystery: unknown mastered by being it — knowledge's instruments returning empty, yet the Immeasurable unthreatened by their failure. After the fourfold antitheses, unknowability crowns: pairs held, yet Holder unheld. Hiddenness here is excess of presence, not absence. Master one mystery today by being present to it. (Stotra verse 51.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-051"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-483", title: "Sahasrāṃśuḥ", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Thousand-rayed. Sahasrāṃśu beams by thousands: light legion, radiance regimental — hiddenness (previous name) blazing, mystery shining. Unrecognized yet thousand-rayed: the verse's deliberate paradox, concealment emitting illumination. Every ray reaches; no beam is wasted. The sun's thousand is His innumerable. Beam once today legionly: shine in many small directions. (Stotra verse 51.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-051"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-484", title: "Vidhātā", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Ordainer and supporter of the world (named again). Vidhātā re-dispenses order: apportioning renewed, justice re-issued — karma's accountant with perfect books, every fruit measured. After hiddenness blazing (previous names), ordaining reassures: mystery administers fairly, radiance reckons rightly. What ripens, ripens by measure. Re-dispense one order today: apportion fairly. (Stotra verse 51.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-051"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-485", title: "Kṛtalakṣaṇaḥ", category: "Altar-Grace", summary: "Marked with purpose, famed for significant marks. Kṛtalakṣaṇa signs meaning: tokens teaching, marks meaning — every characteristic purposeful, every lineament a lesson. The verse closes here so that law, antitheses, hiddenness and radiance all resolve in legibility: the world marked, events signed. Nothing decorative, all declarative. Teach once today tokenly: read one event as marked. (Stotra verse 51.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-051"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-486", title: "Gabhastinemiḥ", category: "Witness", summary: "Hub of the radiant wheel. Gabhastinemi centres cosmos on beams: nave of light, axle of radiance — the sun's circuit pivoted on His stillness, creation wheeling on luminosity. The witness-arc opens with centred brilliance: sattva (next name) given an axle. Every circling has a nave; every wheel a still point. Axle once today luminously: steady one circling thing. (Stotra verse 52.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-052"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-487", title: "Sattvasthaḥ", category: "Witness", summary: "Abiding in sattva's clarity. Sattvastha dwells in lucidity: goodness seated, purity lodged — clarity as climate, joy as weather. Hubbed radiance (previous name) settles here: light becoming character, beams becoming goodness. Yet lion follows: clarity with teeth, gentleness armed. Sattva is not softness but lucidity with spine. Lodge once today lucidly: abide an hour in clarity. (Stotra verse 52.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-052"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-488", title: "Siṃhaḥ", category: "Witness", summary: "Lion-like in majesty (named again). Siṃha re-roars royalty: kingship re-proclaimed, courage crowned — clarity (previous name) given teeth, sattva armed. The early lion (name 200) returns at higher altitude: gentleness grown claws where devotees are threatened. Majesty protects lucidity; teeth guard clarity. Re-proclaim one majesty today: stand unafraid once. (Stotra verse 52.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-052"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-489", title: "Bhūtamaheśvaraḥ", category: "Witness", summary: "Great lord of all beings. Bhūtamaheśvara grounds godhood: deity's depth, divinity deepest — first and ultimate God of everything that breathes. Lion-majesty (previous names) widens here: royalty becoming universality, kingship encompassing kindred. Every being has a great Lord; none is orphan. Ground once today godly: treat one creature as His subject. (Stotra verse 52.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-052"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-490", title: "Ādidevaḥ", category: "Witness", summary: "First deity (named again). Ādideva re-primals: firstness re-firsted, beginning's God re-announced — priority restated lest vastness feel beginningless in the bewildering sense. The great lord (previous name) is also the first: ultimacy with a face at the start. Every lineage has a fountainhead; every worship a first address. Re-first one devotion today: put Him first once. (Stotra verse 52.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-052"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-491", title: "Mahādevaḥ", category: "Witness", summary: "Great deity. Mahādeva magnifies godhood: magnitude worshipped, vastness venerated — greatness as devotion's address, not intimidation's. After firstness (previous name), greatness teaches that the Beginning is also the Boundless: fountainhead as ocean. Rudra's great name stands among Viṣṇu's on purpose: auspiciousness one address. Venerate one vastness today gladly. (Stotra verse 52.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-052"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-492", title: "Deveśaḥ", category: "Witness", summary: "Lord of the shining devas. Deveśa governs brightness: gods supervised, luminaries led — heaven administered, not merely admired. Greatness (previous names) organizes here: vastness given government, glory given order. Every shining thing answers upward; every god has a Lord. Govern one shining thing today kindly: order some brightness. (Stotra verse 52.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-052"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-493", title: "Devabhṛdguruḥ", category: "Witness", summary: "Bearer and teacher of gods — one name, devabhṛt joined to guru. Heaven held up and schooled: divinity carried and coached — the highest carrying its own, then teaching them. Hierarchy folding into service: rank expressing as bearing (bhṛt) and instruction (guru). The verse closes supremacy in tutelage. School one heaven today: teach someone you might merely command. (Stotra verse 52.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-052"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-494", title: "Uttaraḥ", category: "Witness", summary: "Uplifter from the ocean of change. Uttara ferries across: floods crossed, drownings defeated — rescue captained, passage given. The protection-triple opens here: rescue (uttara), tending (gopati), guarding (goptā). Crossing precedes pasturing: first saved, then fed. Every drowning has a Captain. Ferry one flood today: lift someone out. (Stotra verse 53.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-053"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-495", title: "Gopatiḥ", category: "Witness", summary: "Cowherd lord, protector of earth and cattle (named again). Gopati re-pastures protection: herding re-herded mid-rescue-verse, tending between uplifting and guarding. Rescue (previous name) settles into husbandry: saved life fed daily, delivered sheep pastured. Kṛṣṇa's pasture stands behind metaphysics: omnipotence gone pastoral. Re-pasture one protection today: tend something saved. (Stotra verse 53.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-053"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-496", title: "Goptā", category: "Witness", summary: "Protector (named again). Goptṛ re-shelters: guarding re-guarded, shelter re-sheltered — protection triple completed (uplift, herd, guard). After herding (previous name), guarding teaches that tending needs watching: pasture with a fence, kindness with vigilance. Every kept thing has a Keeper awake. Re-shelter one sheltered thing today: check a fence. (Stotra verse 53.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-053"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-497", title: "Jñānagamyaḥ", category: "Witness", summary: "Reached through knowledge. Jñānagamya opens arrival to knowing: destination discoverable by understanding, not by bargaining — wisdom as road, insight as vehicle. Protection (previous names) invites knowing: the guarded may approach, the sheltered may study. The Gītā's knower dear to Him stands behind this name. Yet ancient (next): new to each knower, old as time. Know your way once today: learn deliberately. (Stotra verse 53.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-053"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-498", title: "Purātanaḥ", category: "Witness", summary: "Very ancient. Purātana is age authentic: antiquity actual, oldness original — older than memory, elder to time. After reached-by-knowledge (previous name), ancientness steadies novelty: every discovery is re-discovery, each knower meeting the Always-there. New to each, old as all. Honour one antiquity today: bow to something older than you. (Stotra verse 53.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-053"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-499", title: "Śarīrabhūtabhṛt", category: "Witness", summary: "Bearer of embodied beings and elements — one name, śarīra joined to bhūtabhṛt. Frames freighted: bodies borne, elements shouldered — matter carried from beneath by patience infinite. Knowledge and antiquity (previous names) shoulder here: wisdom bearing weight, age carrying youth. Every step rests on carried ground. Shoulder one frame today: bear someone's bodily burden a little way. (Stotra verse 53.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-053"] },
  { id: "vs-nama-500", title: "Bhoktā", category: "Witness", summary: "Enjoyer (named again). Bhoktṛ re-relishes: enjoyment re-enjoyed, relish renewed — truth tasted in Himself, existence savoured from inside. Bearing frames (previous name) ripens into relishing them: upkeep loving its work, the householder delighting in the house. The world His banquet, not burden. Re-relish one truth today: taste an existence-moment deliberately. (Stotra verse 53.)", relatedVerseIds: ["vs-053"] },
]